term:Best-Efforts Impact Assessment (Kor.deep_dive:
Korea's impact assessment is **best efforts only**, and that softness is the fact the exam tests. **Art. 35** says operators providing high-impact AI **"shall make efforts"** to assess its impact on fundamental rights **in advance**. It is not a mandatory assessment: skipping it carries **no fine**, and it is **not a trigger for fact-finding** under Art. 40 either.

The incentive is procurement, not penalty. **Art. 35(2)** requires national institutions (the state, local governments and public institutions) that use high-impact AI to **give priority to products or services that have been assessed**. So the assessment is voluntary but commercially useful for anyone selling to the Korean public sector.

The **contents** (Decree Art. 28(1)):

- the individuals or groups affected;
- the fundamental rights at stake;
- social and economic impacts;
- usage patterns;
- evaluation indicators and calculation methods;
- measures to prevent and mitigate risks and to recover losses; and
- an improvement plan where one is needed.

Who performs it: **the operator itself, or a third party it commissions** (Decree Art. 28(2)). **Since 21 July 2026** the assessment must also reflect the characteristics of **AI-vulnerable groups** in light of the product or service (Act No. 21311); Decree Art. 1-2 defines those groups, including people with disabilities and people aged 65 and over, and Decree Art. 28(1)1 was amended so that identifying affected groups takes them into account.

The EU contrast is exact. The EU's **fundamental rights impact assessment (Art. 27)** is **mandatory**, but only for certain deployers: bodies governed by public law, private entities providing public services, and deployers of credit-scoring or life and health insurance pricing systems. Korea's is available to all high-impact operators, mandatory for none, and backed by a procurement carrot rather than a fine.

term:AI Importer.deep_dive:
Article 3(6) defines an importer as an entity established in the Union that places on the market an AI system bearing the name or trademark of a provider established outside the Union. The importer is the Union's first point of contact with a foreign provider's system, and Article 23 gives it verification duties rather than design duties.

Before placing a high-risk system on the market, the importer must confirm that:

- the provider has carried out the relevant conformity assessment procedure;
- the provider has drawn up the technical documentation;
- the system bears the CE marking and is accompanied by the EU Declaration of Conformity and instructions for use;
- the provider has appointed an authorised representative in the Union (Article 22).

If the importer has reason to believe the system is not compliant, it must not place it on the market. It must also put its own name and contact address on the system or its packaging, keep the documentation for **10 years**, and cooperate with competent authorities.

Distinguish the importer from the **authorised representative**: the representative is mandated by the provider to act on its behalf, while the importer independently places the goods on the market. Both are separate from the **distributor**, which passes an already-placed system further down the chain.

term:Prohibited AI.deep_dive:
Article 5 of the EU AI Act bans a closed list of practices outright. As enacted (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689) the list has **eight** points, applying since 2 February 2025:

- subliminal or purposefully manipulative techniques that materially distort behaviour and cause significant harm;
- exploitation of vulnerabilities from age, disability or social or economic situation;
- social scoring leading to detrimental or disproportionate treatment;
- individual criminal-offence risk prediction based solely on profiling or personality traits;
- untargeted scraping of facial images to build recognition databases;
- emotion recognition in workplaces and educational institutions;
- biometric categorisation by sensitive traits;
- real-time remote biometric identification in publicly accessible spaces for law enforcement, with three narrow exceptions requiring prior judicial authorisation.

As amended by the Digital Omnibus on AI (Regulation (EU) 2026/1744), two more points, (ba) and (bb), apply from **2 December 2026**.

Prohibited practices sit at the top of the four-tier pyramid and carry the heaviest penalty: up to **EUR 35 million or 7%** of worldwide annual turnover, whichever is higher (Article 99(3)). The distinction the exam draws is between a practice that is **banned entirely** and a high-risk system that is permitted subject to obligations.

term:Design Defect.deep_dive:
A design defect claim says the product was built as intended but the design itself is unreasonably dangerous. The usual test is **risk-utility**: weigh the risk of harm against the product's utility and ask whether a **reasonable alternative design** could have reduced the risk without destroying its usefulness.

Applied to AI, plaintiffs tend to allege one of a small set of failures:

- training data that was insufficient or unrepresentative;
- a safer model architecture that was available and ignored;
- design that omitted human oversight where it was needed;
- a safer default behaviour that was feasible.

Pinning a defect in an adaptive system is hard. Behaviour is context-sensitive, models drift after deployment, and outputs are probabilistic, so a claimant may struggle to show that the design was defective rather than the data, the deployment or the user. The EU's revised Product Liability Directive (EU) 2024/2853 answers part of this: the defect test expressly weighs **the effect of any ability to continue to learn or acquire new features after the product is placed on the market** (Article 7(2)(c)). It also presumes defect where the defendant fails to disclose evidence, the product breaches mandatory safety requirements, or there is an obvious malfunction, and presumes causation where the damage is typical of the defect (Article 10). Machine learning is named as a case of technical complexity that can justify the presumption.

Design defect is distinct from a manufacturing defect (a departure from the design) and from failure to warn (inadequate instructions).

term:Risk Management System (Art. 9).deep_dive:
### The four steps
Article 9(2) makes risk management a loop that runs for the whole life of a high-risk system, with regular systematic review. The provider must:

- identify and analyse known and reasonably foreseeable risks to health, safety or fundamental rights under the intended purpose;
- estimate and evaluate risks under intended use and reasonably foreseeable misuse;
- evaluate further risks shown by post-market monitoring data (Art. 72);
- adopt targeted measures against them.

### Acceptable residual risk
The residual risk of each hazard, and of the system overall, must be judged acceptable (Art. 9(5)). Order of preference: design the risk out where technically feasible, then mitigate and control what remains, then inform and, where appropriate, train deployers.

### Testing and vulnerable groups
Testing runs during development and always before market placement, against metrics and probabilistic thresholds set in advance; it may include real-world testing under Article 60. The provider must consider likely harm to people under 18 and other vulnerable groups (Art. 9(9)). Only risks that design, development or adequate technical information can reasonably reduce are in scope (Art. 9(3)).

term:Instructions for Use (Art. 13).deep_dive:
### Purpose
Article 13(1) requires a high-risk system to be transparent enough that deployers can interpret its output and use it appropriately. The main vehicle is the instructions for use: concise, complete, correct and clear information that deployers can understand.

### Minimum contents (Art. 13(3))
- provider identity and contact details, plus the authorised representative if any;
- intended purpose, and the accuracy (with metrics), robustness and cybersecurity levels the system was tested against, with circumstances that may degrade them;
- foreseeable circumstances, including misuse, that could create risks to health, safety or fundamental rights;
- where relevant, explanation capabilities, performance for specific persons or groups, and input-data specifications;
- changes the provider pre-determined at the initial conformity assessment;
- the Article 14 human oversight measures;
- compute and hardware needs, expected lifetime, maintenance and update frequency;
- how deployers can collect, store and interpret the logs.

### Why deployers care
Deployers must use the system in line with these instructions (Art. 26(1)), monitor against them (Art. 26(5)), and use the information for their GDPR DPIA (Art. 26(9)).

term:Provider Obligations (Art. 16).deep_dive:
### The checklist
Besides building the system to the Section 2 requirements (Arts. 9-15), a provider of a high-risk system must:

- put its name, registered trade name or mark and contact address on the system, packaging or documentation;
- run a quality management system meeting Article 17;
- keep the Article 18 documentation for 10 years and, where under its control, the Article 19 logs;
- complete the relevant conformity assessment (Art. 43) before market placement or putting into service;
- draw up the EU declaration of conformity (Art. 47) and affix CE marking (Art. 48);
- register under Article 49(1);
- take corrective action and inform others under Article 20;
- demonstrate conformity when a national competent authority asks;
- meet EU accessibility requirements.

### Article 20 in brief
A provider with reason to think its system does not conform must immediately bring it into conformity, withdraw, disable or recall it, and tell distributors and, where applicable, deployers, the authorised representative and importers. If the system presents a risk, it investigates with the reporting deployer and informs the market surveillance authority.

### Who becomes a provider
Rebranding a high-risk system, substantially modifying it, or repurposing a system into high-risk use moves these duties onto whoever did it (Art. 25).

term:Deployer Obligations (Art. 26).deep_dive:
### The duties
A deployer is anyone using an AI system under its authority, other than for personal non-professional activity. For a high-risk system, Article 26 requires it to:

- use the system in line with the provider's instructions for use;
- assign human oversight to people with the competence, training, authority and support to do it;
- where it controls input data, keep the data relevant and sufficiently representative for the intended purpose;
- monitor operation; if use may present a risk, inform the provider or distributor and the market surveillance authority without undue delay and suspend use; on a serious incident, inform the provider first, then the importer or distributor and the authority;
- keep the logs it controls for at least six months;
- as an employer, inform workers' representatives and affected workers before using the system at work;
- as a public authority, register its use in the EU database and refuse to use an unregistered system;
- use the Article 13 information for its GDPR DPIA;
- tell people subject to decisions made or assisted by an Annex III system.

### Related duties and timing
Some deployers also owe a FRIA (Art. 27) and must answer explanation requests (Art. 86). Annex III dates: 2 August 2026 as enacted, 2 December 2027 as amended.

term:Serious Incident Reporting (Art. 73.deep_dive:
### What counts
Article 3(49) defines a serious incident as an incident or malfunction of an AI system that directly or indirectly leads to:
- a death, or serious harm to a person's health;
- serious and irreversible disruption of the management or operation of critical infrastructure;
- infringement of EU-law obligations that protect fundamental rights;
- serious harm to property or the environment.

### Deadlines
The provider reports immediately once it establishes a causal link, or a reasonable likelihood of one, and at the latest:
- **15 days** after the provider or deployer becomes aware;
- **10 days** where a person has died, with the report due as soon as a causal link is even suspected;
- **2 days** for a widespread infringement or a critical-infrastructure disruption.

An incomplete initial report may come first. The provider then investigates and corrects, without altering the system in ways that would hamper the inquiry.

### Who tells whom
The report goes to the market surveillance authority of the Member State where the incident happened. A deployer that identifies a serious incident informs the provider first, then the importer or distributor and the authority (Art. 26(5)). Systemic-risk GPAI providers report to the AI Office under Article 55(1)(c).

Carve-outs: medical-device AI, and Annex III providers under equivalent sectoral reporting regimes, report only fundamental-rights infringements under the AI Act.

term:FTC Rite Aid Order (2023).deep_dive:
### What happened

From 2012 to 2020 Rite Aid used AI facial recognition in hundreds of stores to match shoppers against a database of people it suspected of shoplifting. Acting on false alerts, staff followed customers, searched them, ordered them to leave, called the police and accused them publicly. False matches fell disproportionately on women and people of color. On **19 Dec 2023** the FTC charged the practice as **unfair** under FTC Act section 5.

### The failures the FTC listed

- no accuracy testing before deployment, and no request to its two vendors for test results;
- low-quality enrollment images that raised false-match rates;
- no monitoring of false positives after deployment;
- weak training for the staff who acted on alerts.

### The order

- a **5-year ban** on facial recognition for surveillance;
- deletion of the images and of the algorithms and products built from them, with third parties told to delete too (algorithmic disgorgement);
- before any future automated biometric security or surveillance system: risk assessment, accuracy testing, ongoing monitoring and consumer notice.

### Why governance teams cite it

It reads as the FTC's control list for biometric AI: test before use, demand vendor evidence, track errors by demographic group and train the people who act on outputs.

term:Executive Order 14365.deep_dive:
### What it orders

**Executive Order 14365**, Ensuring a National Policy Framework for Artificial Intelligence, was signed **11 Dec 2025** (90 FR 58499). It declares a policy of a minimally burdensome national AI framework and directs agencies to act against state AI laws:

- a **DOJ AI Litigation Task Force** whose only job is to challenge state AI laws on interstate-commerce, preemption or other grounds; the Attorney General's memo of **9 Jan 2026** created it;
- a **Commerce evaluation** within 90 days naming onerous state AI laws, including laws that compel altered outputs or disclosures;
- withholding of **BEAD** non-deployment broadband funds from states with onerous AI laws, as far as federal law allows;
- an **FCC** proceeding on a preemptive federal disclosure standard and an **FTC** policy statement on preemption;
- a legislative recommendation for a preemptive federal framework that spares state child-safety, data-center and state-procurement laws.

### What it cannot do

An executive order cannot preempt state law by itself. Until a court strikes a state law down or Congress acts, laws such as California SB 53 stay enforceable. Congress has passed no moratorium: on **1 Jul 2025** the Senate voted **99-1** to strip one from the budget reconciliation bill. In April 2026 DOJ moved to intervene in xAI's suit against Colorado's SB 24-205.

term:California AB 2013 (Training Data T.deep_dive:
### Who and when

**AB 2013** (Chapter 817, signed 28 Sep 2024) applies to a **developer**: anyone who designs, codes, produces or substantially modifies a generative AI system or service for public use in California. Documentation was due **1 Jan 2026** for systems released on or after **1 Jan 2022**, and is due again before each later release or substantial modification.

### What the website posting covers

- sources or owners of the datasets, and how the data serves the system's purpose;
- number and types of data points;
- whether the data includes copyrighted, trademarked or patented material, or is wholly public domain;
- whether the datasets were purchased or licensed;
- whether they include personal information or aggregate consumer information;
- cleaning and processing, the collection period, first-use dates and any synthetic data.

### Exemptions and enforcement

Systems used only for security and integrity, for operating aircraft, or for national security, military or defense purposes and made available only to a federal entity are exempt. The Act contains no penalty clause of its own.

### Comparison

The EU AI Act requires GPAI model providers to publish a summary of training content (Art. 53(1)(d)). AB 2013 applies to any public generative AI system in California, whatever its size.

term:Colorado AI Act (SB 24-205).deep_dive:
### As enacted

**SB 24-205** was signed **17 May 2024** with a start date of **1 Feb 2026**. It followed a risk-management model:

- it covered developers and deployers of **high-risk AI systems**, meaning systems that make, or are a substantial factor in making, a consequential decision about a consumer;
- both owed a **duty of reasonable care** to protect consumers from known or reasonably foreseeable risks of **algorithmic discrimination**;
- deployers had to adopt a risk-management policy, complete **impact assessments** and review each high-risk system annually;
- developers and deployers had to tell the **attorney general** within **90 days** of discovering algorithmic discrimination or its known or reasonably foreseeable risks;
- businesses had to tell consumers when they were interacting with an AI system.

### What happened

SB 25B-004 (signed 28 Aug 2025) pushed the start date to **30 Jun 2026**. xAI sued to block the law, and on 24 Apr 2026 DOJ moved to intervene. On **14 May 2026** SB 26-189 repealed and re-enacted the statute, so none of SB 24-205's duties ever became enforceable.

### Reading a stem

Older study material still calls SB 24-205 the Colorado AI Act. A stem that says as enacted, or describes a duty of reasonable care and impact assessments, is testing this text. For current Colorado law, use SB 26-189.

term:PLD Exemptions from Liability (Art..deep_dive:
### The defences
Liability is no-fault: the claimant proves defect, damage and causation, never negligence. An economic operator escapes liability only by proving an Art. 11 defence, for example that:
- it did not place the product on the market or put it into service;
- the defect probably arose after placing on the market;
- the defect results from complying with legal requirements;
- the objective state of scientific and technical knowledge could not reveal the defect (development risk);
- as a component maker or modifier, the defect lies outside its part.

### The software exception
Art. 11(2) removes the later-defect defence where the defect comes from a related service, software including updates or upgrades, a lack of updates needed for safety, or a substantial modification, if these are within the manufacturer's control. Development risk is judged at placing on the market or during the period the product stays within the manufacturer's control, and Member States may keep or introduce liability despite it, subject to notification (Art. 18).

### Why it matters for AI
An unsafe over-the-air model update, or a missing safety patch, stays the manufacturer's problem. A substantial modification through an update or continuous learning counts as a fresh placing on the market (recital 40), so keep update logs and check each release for substantial modification.
